<% content_for :title, "Taoism & Bipolar | Bipolar Sober" %>

<style>
  .taoism-bipolar-page .buddhism-copy {
    min-width: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .taoism-bipolar-page .buddhism-source {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-size: 0.75em;
    font-style: normal;
  }

  .taoism-bipolar-page .buddhism-callout {
    margin-block: 2rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1.8;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
</style>

<main class="taoism-bipolar-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="taoism-bipolar-hero">

    <div class="taoism-bipolar-hero-content">

      <p class="taoism-bipolar-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / PERSPECTIVES / TAOISM
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>BIPOLAR</span>
        <span>THROUGH A</span>
        <span>TAOIST LENS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="taoism-bipolar-intro">
        YOU CAN'T FORCE THE CURRENT.<br>
        YOU CAN CHOOSE HOW YOU MEET IT.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 01 / INTRODUCTION -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-pink"
           aria-labelledby="taoism-introduction-title">

    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">
      <p class="buddhism-number">01 / A TAOIST PERSPECTIVE</p>
      <h2 id="taoism-introduction-title">WORK WITH WHAT'S HERE</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">

      <blockquote>
        <p>"The Tao never acts, yet nothing is left undone."</p>
        <cite class="buddhism-source">—Tao Te Ching, chapter 37</cite>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Taoism offers you a way to think about living with change without
        demanding that you control every part of your experience. The Tao,
        often translated as "the Way," points toward the movement and
        patterns of life. Taoist ideas encourage you to notice what is
        happening, understand the conditions around it, and respond without
        adding more force than the situation calls for.
      </p>

      <p>
        Bipolar disorder is a medical condition, not proof that you are out
        of harmony with the universe. Taoism does not replace treatment or
        explain away the realities of mania, depression, or psychosis. It
        can offer another perspective on how you meet those experiences
        and care for yourself through change.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 02 / CHANGE AND CONTRAST -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-yellow"
           aria-labelledby="taoism-change-title">

    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">
      <p class="buddhism-number">02 / CHANGE AND CONTRAST</p>
      <h2 id="taoism-change-title">OPPOSITES CAN COEXIST</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">

      <p>
        Yin and yang describe complementary qualities in the changing world:
        stillness and activity, darkness and light, rest and movement. They
        influence one another, and neither exists in isolation.
      </p>

      <p>
        It can be tempting to map depression onto yin and mania onto yang.
        That comparison has limits. Bipolar episodes are not balanced,
        necessary halves of a spiritual cycle. They can be dangerous and
        deeply disruptive, and you deserve appropriate care for them.
      </p>

      <p>
        The more useful idea is that your experience can change without
        defining your whole identity. You are not more authentically yourself
        when you are manic, and you are not less yourself when depression
        makes it hard to function. Each state affects how you feel and
        interpret the world. Neither tells the whole story of who you are.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 03 / WU WEI -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-black"
           aria-labelledby="taoism-wuwei-title">

    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">
      <p class="buddhism-number">03 / WU WEI</p>
      <h2 id="taoism-wuwei-title">LESS FORCING. MORE DISCERNMENT.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">

      <p>
        Wu wei is often translated as "nonaction" or "effortless action."
        It does not mean giving up, ignoring problems, or refusing
        responsibility. It suggests acting in a way that fits the
        circumstances instead of exhausting yourself by forcing what
        cannot be forced.
      </p>

      <p>
        During depression, you may keep demanding that you function as if
        your energy and capacity were unchanged. If the demand leaves you
        overwhelmed and ashamed, pause and look at what is actually possible
        today. You might simplify a task, accept help, rest, or ask your
        psych for support.
      </p>

      <p>
        During mania, the challenge may be slowing down an urge to act.
        Every idea can feel urgent and every opportunity can seem
        impossible to pass up. Sometimes the fitting response is to wait,
        make fewer commitments, and let someone you trust help you assess
        the decision.
      </p>

      <p>
        Neither response is passive. Both ask you to notice the conditions
        you are in and choose an action that fits them.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 04 / URGENCY AND CERTAINTY -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-purple"
           aria-labelledby="taoism-urgency-title">

    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">
      <p class="buddhism-number">04 / URGENCY AND CERTAINTY</p>
      <h2 id="taoism-urgency-title">A FEELING ISN'T AN ORDER</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">

      <p>
        Taoist humility can help when your mind feels completely certain.
        During mania, a plan or belief may seem urgent, brilliant, or
        unquestionably true. The strength of that feeling does not guarantee
        that your judgment is reliable in that moment.
      </p>

      <p>
        You do not have to treat every thought as false. You can leave room
        for another explanation. Delay a major purchase or commitment.
        Check in with someone who knows your patterns. Bring changes in
        sleep, energy, or thinking to your psych.
      </p>

      <div class="buddhism-callout">
        <p>
          NOTICE THE URGENCY.<br>
          GIVE THE DECISION SOME ROOM.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>
        Accepting help or adding a safeguard does not mean you have failed
        at independence. It means you are taking your own patterns seriously.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 05 / SIMPLICITY -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-pink"
           aria-labelledby="taoism-simplicity-title">

    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">
      <p class="buddhism-number">05 / SIMPLICITY</p>
      <h2 id="taoism-simplicity-title">COME BACK TO THE ORDINARY</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">

      <p>
        Living with bipolar disorder can turn your attention inward all day.
        You monitor your mood, question your thoughts, assess your energy,
        and wonder whether every change means something. Awareness can help,
        but constant analysis can become its own source of strain.
      </p>

      <p>
        Taoist simplicity invites you to return to what is directly in front
        of you. Eat something. Rest. Take your medication as prescribed.
        Spend time with Banjo. Do one manageable task instead of planning
        twelve. You do not have to solve every question about your mind
        before you can participate in your day.
      </p>

      <p>
        Ordinary routines do not need to feel profound to matter. Their
        steadiness can support you through periods when your internal
        experience is anything but steady.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 06 / NOURISHING LIFE -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-yellow"
           aria-labelledby="taoism-nourishing-title">

    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">
      <p class="buddhism-number">06 / CARE AND MAINTENANCE</p>
      <h2 id="taoism-nourishing-title">NOURISH THE LIFE YOU HAVE</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">

      <p>
        Taoist traditions include practices of caring for and nourishing
        life. For you, that idea can mean paying attention to the conditions
        that support your well-being: sleep, food, treatment, manageable
        routines, and connection with people you trust.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is not a promise that a routine will prevent every episode.
        It is a reminder that your mind and body need care even when you
        are busy trying to understand what they are doing. If your usual
        supports are not enough, asking your psych for help is part
        of responding to the situation.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 07 / ACCEPTANCE -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-black"
           aria-labelledby="taoism-acceptance-title">

    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">
      <p class="buddhism-number">07 / ACCEPTANCE</p>
      <h2 id="taoism-acceptance-title">ACCEPTANCE ISN'T APPROVAL</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">

      <p>
        You do not have to like bipolar disorder, call it a gift, or find a
        spiritual reason for it. Acceptance means acknowledging what is
        happening clearly enough to respond. It can make room for treatment,
        boundaries, rest, and changes to a plan.
      </p>

      <p>
        You cannot force perfect stability through willpower. You can still
        make choices that support your health. Sometimes that means acting.
        Sometimes it means stopping, waiting, or letting someone help you
        carry the decision.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 08 / A TAOIST VIEW OF THE PATH -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-purple"
           aria-labelledby="taoism-path-title">

    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">
      <p class="buddhism-number">08 / THE PATH</p>
      <h2 id="taoism-path-title">RESPOND TO THE MOMENT YOU'RE IN</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">

      <p>
        You do not have to treat every change in mood as a command or a
        spiritual message. Notice what is happening, consider what the
        circumstances call for, and choose the next useful step.
      </p>

      <p>
        That step might be rest, a conversation, a delayed decision, an
        appointment, or getting more support. The point is not to drift
        through life without responsibility. It is to respond with enough
        attention to avoid adding unnecessary force to an already difficult
        situation.
      </p>

      <p class="buddhism-callout">
        NOTICE → PAUSE → RESPOND
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- CLOSING -->
  <section class="buddhism-ending"
           aria-labelledby="taoism-closing-title">

    <div>
      <p class="buddhism-ending-small">CLOSING</p>

      <h2 id="taoism-closing-title">
        MEET THE<br>
        <span>MOMENT.</span>
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-ending-copy">

      <p>
        A Taoist perspective does not ask you to enjoy bipolar disorder
        or force yourself into balance. It invites you to notice what is
        changing, care for the life you have, and choose a response that
        fits the moment.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can accept what is here while still seeking treatment and
        working toward greater stability.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO PERSPECTIVES",
          bipolar_perspectives_path,
          class: "buddhism-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>